        "title": "Family and Demographic Change",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Family is the primary institution of reproduction, socialization, and care — but its form is neither universal nor static. Demography provides the quantitative framework: birth rates, death rates, marriage rates, and their long-run trends tell us about structural change in societies. Sociology asks why those numbers shift and what they mean for inequality, care, and the organization of daily life."
    },
    {
      "id": "06-FAMILY-DEMOGRAPHIC-CHANGE.md#003",
      "path": [
